  "response": "Betelgeuse is a red supergiant star in the constellation Orion, and its brightness has been followed by observers for well over a century. Lying close to the boundary between the two star patterns, Betelgeuse is located in the constellation Taurus as well. Its evolutionary state as a bloated, convective red supergiant caused it to begin dimming noticeably in late 2019, because stars of this kind pulsate irregularly and shed mass from an extended, unstable envelope. What turned this decline into a scientific puzzle was its depth and timing. Two rival explanations emerged. One held that the star had expelled a cloud of dust that blocked part of its light. The other held that a large cool region had spread across its surface.\n\nThe episode, soon called the Great Dimming, unfolded over several months. Betelgeuse reached the faintest point of the Great Dimming in February 2020; in other words, the minimum brightness of Betelgeuse during the Great Dimming occurred in February 2020. The late-2019 decline caused Betelgeuse to fade to about 1 percent of its normal brightness during the Great Dimming. At least one of these features of the star, perhaps both, drew unusual public attention: its location in Taurus or that steep fade. Much of the popular discussion turned on a single question. Either Betelgeuse exploded as a supernova in 2020, or Betelgeuse did not explode as a supernova in 2020. The two outcomes cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. For astronomers, however, the more pressing task was to identify the physical mechanism behind the fade. That required data that could locate the light loss on the star itself.\n\nThe first clue came from ultraviolet spectroscopy. Hubble Space Telescope spectra revealed hot, dense material moving outward from Betelgeuse in the autumn of 2019. A cloud of dust partially obscured Betelgeuse's southern hemisphere during the Great Dimming. Montarg\u00e8s and colleagues imaged the surface of Betelgeuse with the SPHERE instrument on the European Southern Observatory's Very Large Telescope. After those observing campaigns, the VLT images showed the dimming concentrated in the southern part of the star's disk.\n\nThe dust account did not, however, exhaust the evidence. A large cool region formed on Betelgeuse's surface during the Great Dimming, even though Levesque and Massey found that Betelgeuse's effective temperature had dropped only modestly during the dimming. On its face, a modest global temperature change appears hard to reconcile with a substantial cool patch. A disk-averaged temperature can nonetheless conceal a localized depression if that depression covers only part of the visible surface. The 2021 Nature study by Montarg\u00e8s and colleagues concluded that the Great Dimming resulted from both a cool surface patch and a newly formed dust cloud.\n\nThe southern concentration in the VLT images indicates this combined explanation. A single uniform cooling would have darkened the whole disk evenly, and a dust cloud alone would not match the measured temperature behavior. The combined model also settles the apparent conflict over temperature. In this picture, a cool patch lowered the temperature of part of the photosphere. That local cooling allowed gas to condense into dust above the southern hemisphere. Because the cooling was regional, the star's global effective temperature fell only modestly, as Levesque and Massey had measured. The dust then supplied the additional extinction needed to explain the depth of the fade. The rival hypotheses were therefore not mutually exclusive. Astronomers established the cause of the dimming by combining Hubble spectroscopy of the outflow, temperature diagnostics of the photosphere, and resolved VLT imaging of the disk. Together, these data showed that each proposed mechanism accounted for part of the event.",
